\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You know the way Ezekiel often brings back themes, ideas, even entire chapters worth of material, but more intensely the second time? Try reading what the previous instance of Claude said about chapters 22 and 23, then try reading Ezekiel 24:1-14, looking carefully at the Hebrew. The bloody city, lewd Jerusalem, refining to remove corruption are not just repeated - they come back in a way that demands we understand their implications.\
In a way, Ezekiel is bringing back the entire book until now, centering around the cauldron metaphor of chapter 11, and the dispute over what that cauldron represents.\
But if we are to hear this parable, for so verse 3 calls it, we must let all the words Ezekiel was given by the LORD speak.
\f0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
