In any case, verse 6 immediately jumps to taking a different approach to describing the city than verses 3-5. These twin descriptions come before the removal of the meat you mentioned, drawing on the guilt for shed blood from chapter 22, and the corruption this passage continues to discuss.\
That corruption has two ways we can read it. Either the metal of the pot is corrupt, or the meat within the pot. \
The meat within reads the preposition 'in' more naturally, and does not require a focus shift in our reading. \
However, in verse 11, the metal of the pot clearly needs cleansed - thus the extreme heating of the copper. This is more natural if the corruption has been in the metal of the pot all along, but contamination going beyond the physical from rotting meat is at least a logical possibility. And whether we read pouring as pouring out melted metal, or pouring out the corrupted meat, remains a parallel interpretive question.\
Either way we read the corruption, the corruption is stubborn, and extreme measures must be taken with the pot, that is, Jerusalem.\
As for setting the blood on bare rock, this is indeed shameless public guilt, and a connection to the bloody city of chapter 22 with its breadth of predatory disregard for human life. But there is also a reference here to Leviticus 17, with its command to pour out the blood of animals to be eaten and cover it with earth. This goes beyond eating animals in the correct way, because of the rationale for the command: the blood is its life. Whether murder, or economic injustice, or child sacrifice, the issue is the same, that blood represents life which is God's alone, and which will cry out to God if taken unjustly.\
Within the analogy of the cooking pot of meat, the contents of the pot are like meat with the blood still in it; they may not be eaten, and it is too late to turn them into something that may be eaten.\
