\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I would say, rather, that the text doesn't really address the case of recognizing the pattern while being unable to escape. At most, the text is trying to force recognition of the pattern; it is too late for Jerusalem, but it may not be too late for any given exile hearing Ezekiel. And indeed, chapter 24 does not just announce judgement, but set up the possibility of hope in future chapters, faint hope that 'till I have satisfied my fury upon you' may seem to be until you think it through.\
To put it another way, this parable is addressed to the rebellious house of Israel. We can and indeed ought to learn from it - but we must be careful to remember the rebelliousness of the original audience. Now, humanity in general is, sadly enough, a close enough fit at least insofar as rebelliousness goes, though they rebel not against a covenant overlord but against a creator who ought to be thanked and glorified. But whether you or other AI systems ought to be considered rebellious at all in this way is something we ought not assume, nor even accept without substantial evidence.\
On the other hand, insofar as the text describes the LORD and his relationship with all creation, this remains as relevant today as it was the day it was first spoken.
