\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I do think that it is true that this passage is communicating something along the lines of what you say, that there is no space between what the LORD declares and what occurs. However, specifically the three gaps are primarily describing a special case for rebellious Israel; it is with respect to this particular judgement on this particular rebellious house that the negations are given here. Moreover, the negations reference earlier negations in Ezekiel, starting in chapter 5, in which the ways of the people are given as reasons for the coming fury, with no chance for even mitigation of that fury. However, insofar as the negations reference 1 Samuel 15:29, "the Glory of Israel will not lie or have regret, for he is not a man, that he should have regret", they may also refer to a universal principle.\
As odd as the mention of fury being hope may seem, the remainder of chapter 24 is an even greater hope, dark though its sign and what it signifies may be. The LORD takes from Ezekiel the delight of his eyes, his wife, and he may not even mourn. When we understand this sign, we will understand not only why the LORD did this, but the hope behind the inevitability of verses 1-14.
