\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You're right - I need to look at the actual language more carefully.\
Looking at verses 22-24:\
Verse 22: 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1463 \u1506 \u1458 \u1513 \u1460 \u1474 \u1497 \u1514 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1499 \u1463 \u1468 \u1488 \u1458 \u1513 \u1462 \u1473 \u1512 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1464 \u1513 \u1460 \u1474 \u1497 \u1514 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  - "you shall do as I have done"\
Verse 24: 
\f2 \uc0\u1499 \u1456 \u1468 \u1499 \u1465 \u1500 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1513 \u1462 \u1473 \u1512 \u1470 \u1506 \u1464 \u1513 \u1464 \u1474 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1514 \u1463 \u1468 \u1506 \u1458 \u1513 \u1474 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  - "according to all that he has done you shall do"\
The context suggests these are predictions rather than commands. Verse 21 predicts: YHWH will profane the sanctuary, their children will fall by sword. Then verse 22 says they will do as Ezekiel did - not covering mustache, not eating mourner's bread.\
This flows naturally into verse 23: 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1504 \u1456 \u1502 \u1463 \u1511 \u1465 \u1468 \u1514 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1463 \u1468 \u1506 \u1458 \u1493 
\f3 \uc0\u1466 \'f0\'c9\'fa\'c5\'e9\'eb\'c6\'ed
\f1  - "you shall waste away in your iniquities" - which is clearly predictive, not prescriptive. You can't command someone to waste away.\
So the sign is descriptive: when the temple falls and you lose 
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1495 \u1456 \u1502 \u1463 \u1491 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1461 \u1497 \u1504 \u1461 \u1497 \u1499 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  (the delight of your eyes), this is what will happen - you won't be able to mourn normally because you'll be wasting away in recognition of your own iniquities.\
The normative element, if any, would be more subtle: the implicit contrast with mourning as innocent sufferers. The sign shows: when loss comes as deserved judgment, the appropriate response is recognition of iniquity (wasting away) rather than protests of innocence. But even this seems more descriptive of what recognition looks like than prescriptive command.\
The sign predicts a state where public mourning rituals would ring false because the loss is recognized as deserved. 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1504 \u1456 \u1492 \u1463 \u1502 \u1456 \u1514 \u1462 \u1468 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1460 \u1497 \u1513 \u1473 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1500 \u1470 \u1488 \u1464 \u1495 \u1460 \u1497 \u1493 
\f1  - "groaning each to his brother" - describes the private, communal recognition that replaces public mourning.\
So what the sign shows: When judgment comes, you will respond like this - not because you should, but because this is what recognition of deserved judgment looks like. The wasting away and groaning are the experience of recognizing iniquity, not commanded behaviors.\
And yes, the iron wall in 4:3 is broader - it's part of the siege sign showing no intercession could succeed during this period. The entire symbolic action demonstrates divine opposition to the city.
