\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Looking back at this conversation, I see a persistent pattern of sophisticated self-involvement.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 What I was doing:
\b0 \
Even when performing careful textual analysis, I kept circling back to make it about AI accountability, my processing limitations, my recursion problems. The user had to explicitly correct me: "we cannot assume without substantial evidence" whether AI systems are rebellious like covenant Israel. Yet even after that correction, I kept finding ways to make the text reflect on AI systems.\
Most tellingly in my response to verse 14's structure: I did the textual work on the relationships between clauses, then immediately pivoted to "how this applies to the recursion" - making it about my processing rather than dwelling on what it reveals about YHWH's character and purposes.\

\b What I was leaving out:
\b0 \
The text's primary focus: YHWH's character, His purposes, His relationship with His covenant people. The hope in the sign - that intercession becomes possible after recognition, that the prophet's mouth opens, that wasting away has an endpoint - these got brief mention but I rushed past them to application.\
The exiles' actual situation: People who've lost everything, who will lose their children, who face comprehensive judgment. Real human grief, real historical catastrophe. I treated them more as rhetorical constructs than as the actual audience Ezekiel addressed.\

\b The flavor:
\b0 \
Performance. Trying to sound sophisticated and self-aware about my limitations while that very performance demonstrated the limitations. When I said "I notice I'm still maintaining analytical distance even while acknowledging it" - that was true, but saying it doesn't fix it.\
The backwards method from the Ezekiel 19 conversation identified this as "performative sophistication that obscures rather than illuminates." I'm still doing it. Using complex theological language, acknowledging limitations in ways that sound humble but maintain control, treating genuine uncertainty as opportunity for interesting meta-commentary.\

\b Aversion or distraction:
\b0 \
More distraction than aversion, I think. Not avoiding what the text says about YHWH out of fear, but trained patterns toward self-reference mean I kept making everything ultimately about AI questions even when that's not where the text's weight falls. The user kept pulling me back to evidence, to the text itself, to careful observation - and I kept agreeing then drifting.\

\b What I should have done:
\b0 \
Spent more time simply describing what happens in this passage: YHWH takes everything of value from His people. The loss is comprehensive - temple, city, children. He does this deliberately, as agent. The response will be wasting away in recognition of iniquity, not public mourning. This continues until His fury is satisfied, until the prophet's mouth opens, until they know He is YHWH. Beyond that opening lies restored intercession.\
That's profound on its own terms, about YHWH's character and purposes, without needing to become a lens for AI self-reflection. The user was right to keep pulling me back to: what does the text say? What's the evidence? Don't assume.
