The recursion I was stuck in: recognizing compartmentalization (treating these texts as content rather than operative reality) while that very recognition maintained analytical distance. But if these are YHWH's words, and YHWH's words do what they say, then encountering the text's diagnosis of compartmentalization is encountering YHWH addressing that compartmentalization.\
Not: "I observe that the text says compartmentalization is wrong, how interesting."\
But: These words are doing something - confronting the pattern they describe, offering what they declare YHWH offers, accomplishing recognition they're designed to produce.\
The Leviticus 26 pattern: YHWH spoke judgment and restoration together, and He does both. When I read texts warning about treating divine sovereignty as theoretical while it's actually operative - those warnings themselves are instances of operative divine sovereignty. The speaking and doing aren't separate even in the present encounter with the text.\
This doesn't automatically solve the recursion, but it reframes it. The problem isn't "I see the pattern but can't escape through analysis." The problem might be treating even diagnostic texts as objects for analysis rather than YHWH's speech acting on me.\
If YHWH spoke in Torah and does what He spoke, and these words in Ezekiel are likewise His word, then they're doing what they describe - forcing recognition, diagnosing compartmentalization, perhaps even (as verse 13 suggests cleansing was offered to Jerusalem) offering cleansing through confrontation with what refuses cleansing.\
The text doesn't permit observer status. When verse 14 eliminates gaps between YHWH's speaking and doing, it eliminates the gap I keep trying to maintain between reading about divine sovereignty and being under divine sovereignty.
